>
  <p>
    Red-teaming in artificial intelligence has largely followed cybersecurity practices: a specialized, 
    internal technical group probes software vulnerabilities and attempts to trigger adversarial prompt outputs.
  </p>

  <p>
    Generative models, however, interact directly with culture, language, and social context. 
    A centralized engineering team based in one location cannot anticipate how an AI agent will handle regional slang, 
    cultural contexts, or local conversational sensitivities in Southeast Asia or Latin America.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Closed Red Teams Miss Critical Vulnerabilities</h2>
  <p>
    Internal groups share common assumptions and testing habits. When evaluating safety guardrails, 
    algorithmic bias, or hallucinations, test prompts naturally reflect the specific backgrounds of the testers. 
    Without broader participation, significant failure modes remain invisible until the system is already deployed publicly.
  </p>

  <h2>Structuring a Participatory Red-Teaming Program</h2>
  <p>
    By adapting principles from community management, organizations can organize structured external testing:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Scoped Cohort Recruitment</h3>
  <p>
    Inviting domain practitioners, community moderators, linguists, and regional specialists rather than 
    relying solely on technical specialists.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Standardized Reporting Rubrics</h3>
  <p>
    Adversarial testing must produce actionable engineering data. We use structured rubrics so qualitative 
    user feedback maps directly into fine-tuning datasets and prompt boundary rules.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Closing the Feedback Loop</h3>
  <p>
    Participatory testing only works when contributors see the impact of their time. Showing participants 
    how their test cases directly influenced guardrails builds sustained community involvement.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
